  "name": "policy-prefetch-failure",
  "description": "Comparison harness for a policy that is expected to help the mean and hurt the tail. Run once with predictive_prefetch and once with hot_cold using the same seed and trace; the hypothesis is that aggressive prefetching lowers mean decode latency but saturates the node-to-pool links and raises p99. This is a hypothesis to verify, not a result. It is not a measured-hardware experiment.",
  "assumptions": [
    "Each compute-to-pool link is modeled as an independent byte server. Shared pool service contention is not yet modeled in this revision, so treat absolute queue depths as provisional.",
    "Aggressive prefetch parameters are deliberately set to expose the failure mode: long lookahead, low confidence threshold, and a high in-flight cap.",
    "The workload is synthetic KV-cache decode derived from Llama-3-70B geometry, not a captured serving trace.",
    "Values are starting presets from docs/HARDWARE-MODELS.md. Replace them with measured values before making absolute claims.",
    "The expected result is conditional: prefetching should win when accesses are predictable and the link is lightly loaded, and lose when prefetch traffic competes with demand."
  ],
